{
  "dom_id": "step-52855",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Whenever we have a fraction in an equation, we apply the multiplication principle to get rid of it. For example, consider the equation below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+12}{3} =6\n$$\n\nSince the opposite of dividing by $3$ is multiplying by $3$, we apply the multiplication principle and multiply both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2x+12}{3} &=6\\\n\t\t3 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{3}\\right) &= 3 \\cdot 6\\\t\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{2x+12}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) &= 18 \\\t\n\t\t2x+12 &= 18\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe process of removing the fraction is called **clearing the rational expression**. We now have an equation that we can solve using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x+12 &= 18\\\n\t\t2x+12-12 &=18-12 \\   \n\t\t2x &=6 \\   \n\t\t\\dfrac{2x}{2} &=\\dfrac{6}{2} \\\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$",
